WEBVTT

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:03.530
Three colleagues are launching a service for business partners.

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:05.680
Their agents are making fast progress.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:10.240
But each colleague has a different idea of ready: self-service,

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:13.680
a staffed pilot, or a reliable system behind the scenes.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:17.160
The Converge methodology helps them turn those assumptions

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.200
into a shared bet: get the first partners working,

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:23.780
learn before expanding, and stay within agreed limits.

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:26.480
One colleague owns the partner experience.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.120
Another owns access and approvals.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.440
A third owns running the service.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:35.360
Each can carry a substantial contribution with agent support.

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:38.940
They name an owner for the combined result, agree

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:41.880
the promises between them, and make clear which decisions

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:44.380
stay local and which need the affected owners.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:48.900
Nobody has to supervise every detail of everybody else's work.

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:54.060
Their agents put the different launch assumptions side by side.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:56.540
Self-service could reach more partners, but

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:59.120
needs more work before anyone can start.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:03.780
A staffed pilot reaches fewer people and gets feedback sooner.

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:07.400
Agents prepare a working mock and a recommendation.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:11.680
The owners choose the pilot because learning comes first.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.960
Unaffected work continues while they settle that shared direction.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:20.040
A shared contract records a promise the workstreams can rely on:

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.640
a request keeps a responsible team until the next team accepts it.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:28.300
They agree who records that change and what partners see.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.980
A chosen interface or exact experience behavior

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:34.960
can belong here when others depend on it.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:37.300
Ordinary internal choices stay with the owner.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.820
Each agent workflow gets the same current agreement.

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:46.180
The experience owner asks a designer to try the handoff mock.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.920
The agent supplies the goal, the promise, and the open question.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:54.500
She spots the problem: the screen says

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:57.480
transferred before the next team accepts.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:00.740
A partner could think help is on its way when it isn't.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:05.000
They change it to transfer requested, keep the current

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:08.240
team visible, and give agents that example to implement.

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:12.620
A useful update helps peers make the next investment decision.

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:14.600
The pilot is ready for a small group.

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:17.320
A wider launch needs more support coverage.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:20.560
We recommend learning from this group before expanding.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.670
The evidence and alternatives are one step away.

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:26.640
Colleagues can judge the trade-off without reconstructing every

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:30.790
task, and owners keep making choices within their existing authority.

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:33.780
People and agents make choices within the authority already given.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:36.540
A consequential delegated choice gets

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:38.320
proportionate investigation and checks.

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:42.820
Changing a promise another owner relies on needs that owner's agreement.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:46.680
Agents prepare those decisions and keep independent work moving.

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:51.340
If everyone needs the same expert at once, the team reprioritizes

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:54.160
commitments and protects time for the work that needs real thought.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:59.880
Now agents test the corrected experience across the real contributions.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:00.540
Request a transfer.

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:03.440
Decline it: the original team still owns it.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:07.040
Accept it: responsibility changes, and the partner sees the new team.

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:10.380
This checks that the agreed promise survived implementation.

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:12.980
Agents repair any mismatch and repeat the journey.

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:16.160
The team receives evidence of the whole working together.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:18.640
The owners judge whether the pilot serves its

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:20.680
purpose and whether the evidence deserves confidence.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:24.740
They decide what to expand, adjust, or stop within their authority.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:28.080
They also examine the total attention it consumed,

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:31.600
including setup, review, corrections, and later repair.

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:33.660
Which corrections repeated?

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:35.520
Did advice reach the product?

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:37.700
Was someone left to rescue the result?

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:41.160
Use those answers to improve the next cycle of teamwork.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.260
Try the Converge methodology on work you already share.

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:48.740
Give your agent the guidance and your project.

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:53.740
Ask for a short agreement: one useful outcome, the owners,

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:56.480
the promises between them, and who checks the whole.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:03:59.560
Settle consequential differences with your peers.

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:03.120
Let owners make the choices already within their authority.